  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThe summer heat will drag on, though not quite as hot as earlier\nthis week. For Thursday and Friday, an area of upper level high\npressure will continue to be centered over western New Mexico. \nThis ridge will keep our high temperatures running 5 to 10 degrees\nabove average. That will keep lowland high temperature at or a \nlittle above the triple digit mark. Our rain chances will continue\nto be limited, with the best chances for rain in area mountains. \nLike tonight, a few thunderstorms will create some gusty outflow \nwinds which could move off quite a distance from their parent \nthunderstorms.\n\nFor the weekend, the upper level ridge will begin an eastward jog.\nBut the eastward movement is not enough to help set up a deep\nmonsoon flow across our region. The majority of the monsoon \nmoisture will be pumped into central and eastern Arizona. We will \ncontinue to stay mainly dry with a chance for rain in area \nmountains and west of the Continental Divide. High temperatures \nboth afternoons will run 4 to 8 degrees above average. \n\nFor the start of next week, the main section of the upper level\nridge is well to our east, but it will extend back to the west and\ninto our region in an elongated fashion. So despite some moisture\nnear the surface, we will continue to see limited rain chances.\nHigh temperature will continue to be 3 to 7 degrees above average.\nAs we head into September the tropical Pacific begins to get\nmore active. The position of the upper level ridge will make all \nthe difference in whether we are able to pull some of the tropical\nmoisture in our direction or if all the tropical moisture and \nenergy heads on off out into the Pacific.",
